WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.465
Equilibrium propagation is a promising energy-based
alternative to backpropagation for

00:00:05.545 --> 00:00:09.333
training neural networks on brains or neuromorphic
hardware,

00:00:09.413 --> 00:00:15.136
but it classically demands two things physical
substrates cannot easily deliver: perfectly

00:00:15.216 --> 00:00:18.424
symmetric weights and infinitesimally small nudges.

00:00:18.504 --> 00:00:24.033
This ICLR 2024 paper from the Friedrich Miescher
Institute and the University of Basel,

00:00:24.113 --> 00:00:26.548
by Axel Laborieux and Friedemann Zenke,

00:00:26.628 --> 00:00:32.350
cleanly separates the two resulting sources of
gradient bias and shows how to remove them.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:36.412
Finite-nudge bias is eliminated exactly with a
Cauchy integral,

00:00:36.492 --> 00:00:42.021
while weight-asymmetry bias is tamed by a new
homeostatic loss that penalizes asymmetry

00:00:42.101 --> 00:00:44.600
of the Jacobian rather than the weights.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:46.599
The payoff: for the first time,

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:52.337
equilibrium propagation trains asymmetric networks
on ImageNet 32 by 32 with only a small

00:00:52.417 --> 00:00:54.400
gap to the ideal symmetric case.
